# vim: set ft=crontab:
# The cron update below prunes orphaned Shdeps dependencies itself: since dot
# 20261003-182615-26e65791, `dot update --cron` prunes by default, under dot's
# update lock and only after repository sync and overlay links succeeded, so a
# failing dependency or post hook no longer stops orphan cleanup. A host still
# on an older dot skips pruning until its next run upgrades dot; set
# DOT_SHDEPS_PRUNE=never on this line to opt a host out.
# `--force` makes the provider and hooks re-check every dependency on each
# run instead of honoring their per-dependency update caches, so a new release
# reaches every host within one 30-minute interval. This deliberately reverses
# #250: it also overrides a `pinned` shdeps_update_policy, so drop it before
# switching this fleet to `pinned`.
# Cron starts jobs with a bare /bin/sh environment that never loads env.d, so
# without this the update would get no GH_TOKEN and Shdeps would fall back to
# anonymous GitHub API calls. Those share one 60-per-hour quota across every
# host behind the same public IP, and a rate-limited run reports dependencies
# as current instead of updating them. Read the same token file that
# env.d/60-github.sh exports (a plain file read: `gh auth token` from cron
# once leaked keyring daemons), and keep any GH_TOKEN the caller already set.
*/30 * * * * GH_TOKEN="${GH_TOKEN:-$(head -n 1 "$HOME/.config/gh/github-pat" 2>/dev/null)}" dot update --cron --force
